WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.944
An equity analysis asks more than whether a change

2
00:00:03.967 --> 00:00:07.500
sounds reasonable to the people proposing it. It asks

3
00:00:07.522 --> 00:00:10.500
how the decision may be experienced by people who

4
00:00:10.522 --> 00:00:13.811
use, carry out or are otherwise affected by the

5
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:14.100
work.

6
00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:17.812
In the reminder example, the team can begin by

7
00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:21.546
asking which methods people can receive and use, whether

8
00:00:21.568 --> 00:00:24.412
the information is understandable and whether there is a

9
00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:27.168
usable way to ask for help. These are questions

10
00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.301
to explore. They are not conclusions about a person

11
00:00:30.323 --> 00:00:34.812
because of their age, disability, language, location or community.

12
00:00:35.566 --> 00:00:38.966
If you are using your own example, identify the

13
00:00:38.988 --> 00:00:41.499
people affected by the decision and the people who

14
00:00:41.521 --> 00:00:44.877
help carry it out. Their perspectives may be different,

15
00:00:45.366 --> 00:00:48.121
and no single person represents every experience.

16
00:00:49.557 --> 00:00:52.357
The team may know that it currently sends reminders

17
00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:55.890
by mail and is considering another method. It may

18
00:00:55.912 --> 00:00:59.401
know what information appears on a public reminder. It

19
00:00:59.423 --> 00:01:02.401
does not automatically know whether a particular person has

20
00:01:02.423 --> 00:01:06.423
a phone, reads texts, shares a device, understands a

21
00:01:06.446 --> 00:01:07.912
message or prefers mail.

22
00:01:08.493 --> 00:01:11.493
A response count can be useful, but it does

23
00:01:11.516 --> 00:01:14.293
not explain a person's reason for responding or not

24
00:01:14.316 --> 00:01:20.138
responding. Keep observations, interpretations and questions separate. For example:

25
00:01:20.471 --> 00:01:23.049
the team recorded that a reminder was sent; it

26
00:01:23.071 --> 00:01:25.627
still needs to learn whether the person received and

27
00:01:25.649 --> 00:01:26.299
could use it.

28
00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:30.443
Look for existing information that you are authorized to

29
00:01:30.777 --> 00:01:34.599
use. Describe its source, period and limits. If information

30
00:01:34.621 --> 00:01:36.954
is missing, say so. Do not fill a gap

31
00:01:36.977 --> 00:01:38.354
with a confident-sounding guess.

32
00:01:39.356 --> 00:01:42.844
Ask which choices can still be influenced before seeking

33
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:46.267
input. Explain the purpose of the question, the options

34
00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:50.200
under consideration and any known limits. Use the established

35
00:01:50.222 --> 00:01:53.689
engagement process and involve the people or offices responsible

36
00:01:53.711 --> 00:01:55.244
for arranging participation.

37
00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:01.189
Consider what makes participation usable: clear language, accessible materials,

38
00:02:01.722 --> 00:02:04.878
appropriate language assistance, timing, a choice of ways to

39
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.944
respond and support where needed. The right arrangements depend

40
00:02:07.967 --> 00:02:10.544
on the people and the work. Ask rather than

41
00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:10.944
assume.

42
00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:14.933
Be clear about what happens next. Who will review

43
00:02:14.956 --> 00:02:18.000
the input? How will people hear what was considered,

44
00:02:18.156 --> 00:02:21.600
what changed or did not change, and why? Meaningful

45
00:02:21.622 --> 00:02:24.511
participation includes a way to close that loop, even

46
00:02:24.533 --> 00:02:27.178
when every suggestion cannot be adopted.

47
00:02:28.343 --> 00:02:31.188
An individual learner does not need to collect sensitive

48
00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:34.743
information to practice. Keep learning notes general and leave

49
00:02:34.766 --> 00:02:39.010
out names, appointment details, case information, health information and

50
00:02:39.032 --> 00:02:39.877
personnel records.

51
00:02:40.666 --> 00:02:44.666
For actual work, use approved sources, permissions and privacy

52
00:02:44.688 --> 00:02:49.443
practices. Ask the responsible data owner about access, reporting

53
00:02:49.621 --> 00:02:53.154
and small groups before using or sharing information. The

54
00:02:53.177 --> 00:02:54.888
goal is to learn enough to make a better

55
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:57.488
decision, not to gather more detail than the work

56
00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.221
calls for.

57
00:02:58.836 --> 00:03:01.769
When evidence is incomplete, say what it can and

58
00:03:01.791 --> 00:03:05.436
cannot support. A few experiences can reveal important questions

59
00:03:05.458 --> 00:03:09.547
without proving how every person experiences a service. Missing

60
00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:12.724
perspectives are a reason to plan further inquiry, not

61
00:03:12.747 --> 00:03:14.991
a reason to pretend the analysis is complete.

62
00:03:15.958 --> 00:03:19.269
In the activity below, consider what the reminder team

63
00:03:19.291 --> 00:03:23.447
can reasonably conclude from its current information. Choose a

64
00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:26.424
response and then read the feedback. Compare the options

65
00:03:26.447 --> 00:03:29.380
and notice the difference between an observation and an

66
00:03:29.402 --> 00:03:29.824
assumption.

67
00:03:30.538 --> 00:03:33.671
You can pause here and make a brief note

68
00:03:33.693 --> 00:03:37.716
about your own example. What do you know, where

69
00:03:37.738 --> 00:03:41.293
did it come from, whose perspective is missing and

70
00:03:41.316 --> 00:03:43.293
who can help you seek it appropriately?

71
00:03:45.669 --> 00:03:49.113
The next step is to compare options, not to

72
00:03:49.136 --> 00:03:52.313
treat the first attractive idea as the only choice.

73
00:03:52.847 --> 00:03:56.558
Bring the information you have, identify what remains uncertain,

74
00:03:56.913 --> 00:03:59.047
and keep the people affected in view as you

75
00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:00.180
consider the tradeoffs.
